title: Storage open questions
tags: Storage
kind: question
audience: dev
status: proposed
summary: What the storage work of November 2026 left to look at again — replace and rename as ordinary operations, and compressing what the log keeps of a deletion.

Raised while building the tests of undo and redo ([[The operation log]], `tests/undo_redo_properties.rs`) and the change of the value partition to postings ([[Sorting and postings]]). None is decided, except where an item says so.

* ''Replacing a value and renaming a field are not mass writes.'' `PATCH /metarecords/:uuid/fields/:name` and `POST …/fields/:name/rename` write one row at a time (`replace_owned_row`), and so does `retype_field`: a replacement of a value on every match of a query is as many operations as records, in the log and in the store. The batch endpoint (`query/fields/batch`) only runs the four mass writes in one transaction. To see again: whether a replacement (a remove then an add) and a rename (an unset then a set) should be one log entry each, or composed from the mass writes, and what each reads as in the log.
* ''Compressing what the log keeps of a deletion'' (user, 17 November 2026). A deletion keeps the rows of its record in the before of its log entry, in the compact value form; the other effects keep theirs grouped by value (nothing for a remove, the targets that held each value for a set or an unset, [[Log storage]]). A block of consecutive records of a mass deletion, compressed with a general algorithm, may be cheap enough to write the rows when the deletion is made, which would remove the dependence of a pending deletion given back on the state of its records, which its pending `delete` and a copy at the first write settle without writing the rows at the deletion ([[Mass operations in the log]]). Measured on 24 November 2026 on a repository of `benchmarks/bench_data_big` (50 201 records, with their content hashes), its 50 000 files deleted in one write then folded: the before of a chunk, already grouped by value (226 bytes a record, against 336 for its rows), compresses 3.3 times with zstd -1 and 2.2 times with lz4 -1, a whole chunk 2.9 times; zstd -1 compresses at about 500 MB/s and decompresses at about 900 MB/s. The uuids of a chunk are random and do not compress; neither does the chunk of an individual operation, 27 bytes on average. The names of that tree are generated, so a real one may give less. (A first measure, of the records' rows by blocks of 4096, gave 6.3 times: it counted again what the grouping already shares.) To see: the cost of reading one record of a block. Compressing the values of every table is given up (below).
* ''Compressing the store to read faster: given up'' (user, 24 November 2026). LMDB stores values as they are, and a read is a pointer into the mapped file ([[Storage]]): values compressed by the daemon would be decompressed at every read, those already in the page cache too, so the warm case pays to help only the cold one. The whole `data.mdb` of the L bench compresses 2.8 times with lz4 -1 and 3.8 times with zstd -1, free and half-full pages included, so value by value would gain less: not enough. Only a compressing filesystem (btrfs `compress=zstd`) decompresses on disk reads alone, the page cache keeping the pages decompressed; that is the user's choice of filesystem, not the store's.
